content:
A drive is a document, and its model type `powerhouse/document-drive` defines a set of *structural* actions: `ADD_FILE`, `ADD_FOLDER`, `MOVE_NODE`, `DELETE_NODE`. Every one of them is an operation appended to **the drive's** operation log — never to the log of the file being added, moved, or removed.

This is the part that trips people up. When you create a document, you dispatch `ADD_FILE` against the drive:

```ts
// This action is dispatched on the drive, not on the new document
addActions({
  documentId: "<drive-id>",
  actions: [{
    type: "ADD_FILE",
    input: {
      documentType: "my-org/todo-list",
      name: "Shopping list",
      parentFolder: null,
    },
  }],
});
```

The new document exists because the *drive's* reducer processed that action. The document's own log starts empty.

The symmetry holds on the way out. Removing a file appends `DELETE_NODE` to the drive; the removed document's log ends wherever it ended before removal. It has no way to record its own departure, and there is no separate audit document — the drive's log *is* the record of every structural change. Reasoning about "which log records this?" has one answer: membership and layout live on the drive, content lives on the file.

One precision worth keeping: `DELETE_NODE` removes drive *membership*. Deleting the underlying document is a distinct base action, `DELETE_DOCUMENT` (`packages/shared/document-model/types.ts`), which targets the document itself. Dropping a node from a drive is not the same operation as destroying the document.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/03-document-models/01-documents-and-drives.mdx, confirmed against packages/shared/document-model/types.ts (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
